Navigated back to the list (page still transitioning?). The title shows the list. Let me wait for /new to load properly then fill + click. I'll do the whole add in one evaluate that awaits navigation and form readiness, sets values with input events, clicks the Add secret button, waits, and reports. But evaluate may be interrupted by navigation — return before navigation completes: kick off an async IIFE that does the work after delays; evaluate returns immediately. Then poll separately.

Let me write: evaluate (fire-and-forget):
```
(async () => {
  location.href = ".../new";
  await new Promise(r => setTimeout(r, 3000));
  const name = document.querySelector("#secret_name");
  if (!name) return "no-form";
  const setVal = (el, v) => { el.value = v; el.dispatchEvent(new Event("input", {bubbles:true})); el.dispatchEvent(new Event("change", {bubbles:true})); };
  setVal(name, "NBAQ_ODDS_API_KEY");
  setVal(document.querySelector("#secret_value"), "...");
  const btn = [...document.querySelectorAll("button")].find(b => /add secret/i.test(b.innerText));
  btn.click();
  await new Promise(r => setTimeout(r, 3000));
  return {url: location.href, err: document.querySelector(".flash-error")?.innerText || null};
})()
```
evaluate waits for promise; navigation may kill the context mid-await. Instead: do steps in separate evaluate calls with tabs.list polling between.

Step A: navigate to /new (evaluate location.href).
Step B: poll tabs.list until url includes "/new" and title "Add Actions secret".
Step C: fill fields + dispatch events + click button (single evaluate; button click triggers async submit then navigation — evaluate returns before nav completes).
Step D: poll list page until url is the list; check flash-error or secret listed.

